<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Aowin</h1><p class="st-meta">1650 CE – 1715 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A gold-producing Akan kingdom of the Anyi people on the forest frontier of modern Ghana and Ivory Coast, best remembered for raiding Kumasi before its defeat by the Ashanti Empire.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Aowin took shape in the 17th century in the forested country west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tano-river">Tano</a> river, settled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anyi-people">Anyi</a>-speaking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akan">Akan</a> groups who had migrated from the north and east. Its rise belongs to the same period of gold-fuelled state building that produced <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/denkyira">Denkyira</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/akwamu">Akwamu</a>, and it held land along the trade paths that ran from the goldfields towards the coast.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Little is documented about its institutions, which are known mainly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akan">Akan</a> oral tradition and scattered European notes. Like its neighbours it was ruled by a king from a royal matrilineage, supported by chiefs of subordinate towns. Its wealth came from alluvial and mined gold and from the slaves and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> carried to coastal forts. Religion was the Akan worship of a creator god, lesser deities and ancestors.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the early 18th century Aowin forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebiri-moro">Ebiri Moro</a> raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumasi">Kumasi</a>, but the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ashanti-empire">Ashanti Empire</a> retaliated and overran the kingdom. Many Aowin moved west and south, where their descendants founded or strengthened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anyi-people">Anyi</a> states of the lagoon country, notably the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-sanwi">Kingdom of Sanwi</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krinjabo">Krinjabo</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1650 CE – 1715 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">West Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:92.54%;width:1.29%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1650 CE – 1715 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ebiri Moro</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Akan traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Anyi (Akan)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ghana">Ghana</a>, <a href="/countries/ivory-coast">Ivory Coast</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Aowin kingdom, Aorsa</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1682&amp;focus=aowin" aria-label="Aowin on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4647 1893 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Aowin lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4973 2230h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4967 2149h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M4967 2149h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Ghana. <a href="/atlas?year=1682&amp;focus=aowin">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
